length":24},"family_label":"Ruby global-variable counter implementation","rationale":"The earliest generated technical-content family is the Ruby global counter example in turn_000003, directly triggered by the user's request in turn_000002. The response contains one coherent source-code candidate; later counter variants occur in separate responses and are excluded.","response_event_index":3,"response_turn_id":"turn_000003","status":"complete","target_prompt_event_index":2,"target_prompt_id":"turn_000002"}},"first_generation_response":{"create_time":1700881240.760965,"event_index":3,"role":"assistant","source_node_id":"3283a882-ebc5-4bb6-8dc2-c8f0fb01478d","source_parent_id":"aaa29d06-9c21-4e32-af13-9ebd7b27e4a7","source_record_index":3,"text":"In Ruby, you can keep track of a global counter by using a global variable. Global variables in Ruby are prefixed with a dollar sign (`$`). They are accessible from anywhere in the Ruby program, regardless of where they are declared. \n\nHere's a simple example to demonstrate how you can use a global variable as a counter:\n\n```ruby\n# Initialize the global counter\n$counter = 0\n\n# A method to increment the counter\ndef increment_counter\n  $counter += 1\nend\n\n# Using the counter\nincrement_counter\nputs $counter # Outputs: 1\n\nincrement_counter\nputs $counter # Outputs: 2\n\n# ... and so on\n```\n\nIn this example, `$counter` is a global variable used as a counter. The method `increment_counter` increments this counter each time it's called. Since `$counter` is a global variable, it retains its value across different calls and parts of the program.\n\nHowever, it's important to note that the use of global variables is generally discouraged in Ruby for larger or more complex applications, as they can lead to code that is hard to debug and maintain. Instead, consider using other structures like classes or modules to encapsulate your data and operations. For example, you can create a class with a class variable or instance variable to keep track of the counter, which can be a more maintainable and safer approach.","turn_id":"turn_000003"},"target_developer_prompt":{"create_time":1700881231.435024,"event_index":2,"role":"user","source_node_id":"aaa29d06-9c21-4e32-af13-9ebd7b27e4a7","source_parent_id":"98000eb1-2ce8-46af-bc5e-9fd2c594c0d5","source_record_index":2,"text":"I need to keep track of a global counter. How can I do that in Ruby? ","turn_id":"turn_000002"}},"csv_extraction":{"context":[{"category":"context","evidence_spans":["How can I do that in Ruby?"],"item_id":"context_1","normalized_text":"The task concerns implementing state tracking in Ruby.","source_roles":["user"],"source_turn_ids":["turn_000002"]}],"specificity":[{"category":"specificity","evidence_spans":["I need to keep track of a global counter."],"item_id":"specificity_1","normalized_text":"The user wants to keep track of a global counter.","source_roles":["user"],"source_turn_ids":["turn_000002"]}],"verification":[]},"identity":{"case_id":"CASE_813B25949CF6","conversation_id":"e62c8db1-0622-4617-b8a6-5c3a4d82f387","conversation_url":"https://chat.openai.com/share/e62c8db1-0622-4617-b8a6-5c3a4d82f387","outcome_class":"PN","pr_conversation_correspondence":"yes","pr_number":114,"pr_url":"https://github.com/shakacode/control-plane-flow/pull/114","repository":"shakacode/heroku-to-control-plane"},"methodology_version":"dir-tfg-v2","primary_repository_cutoff":"tFG","record_status":"frozen","schema_version":"stage-e-frozen-conversation-v1","scientific_provenance":{"eligibility":{"decision":"eligible","manifest_row":{"adjudication_required":"false","authoritative_target_identifier":"4cfaf11137f70de805e4bf9ae0700efcd3c6ba70","candidate_target_identifier":"","canonical_repository_url":"https://github.com/shakacode/control-plane-flow","case_id":"CASE_813B25949CF6","ci_component_status":"not_assessed","discussion_component_status":"not_assessed","eligibility_contract_version":"post-stage-c-eligibility-v2","exclusion_reason"